WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.409 --> 00:00:14.759
BLAIR: Well, I think was well worth

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:20.769
the rest of it, Nick. 

00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:22.529
Listen, when you talk about a

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:24.480
demeaning political 

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.269
discourse, try the British House of Commons:
the Prime Minister's question time.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:34.720
It kind of defines a demeaning political discourse, I would say.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.029
WOLTERSTORFF: I take it you sometimes felt yourself demeaned there?

00:00:38.029 --> 00:00:43.250
BLAIR: I very seldom felt myself anything else. 

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.520
Listen, here's some good news-- because we've
we've had with us some of

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.330
the people from the other universities
who are now taking this course

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:56.260
from-- from around the world, and actually
the things that we're debating here are

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.370
things that they are debating too,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.519
in a very lively and interesting way,
and so I think what--

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:02.390
it's-- it's

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.480
kind of good to think that we're a part of a broader movement.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.440
And actually what I want to do is to build
very much on what

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.030
Nick has just said, because I'd just like to
say I agree with it.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.370
I just agree with it.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.930
And I want to try to take this debate

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:22.090
to the next stage because I think that

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.729
the classic divide--

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:28.330
and I found this reading the literature
for--

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.630
for what we're going to do today--

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.620
the classic

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.170
divide

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.620
in this debate is

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.820
on the one side, religious people saying, "We've
got the right to enforce our views against--

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.350
against you,"

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.860
and then the secular liberals

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.450
come back and say, "Keep your religion

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.330
out of the public realm." And

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.200
it's then-- when we,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.670
we analyze these things, it's not just about

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.689
the constitution, because

00:01:56.689 --> 00:01:59.960
there can be all sorts of

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.549
things that are outside the

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:04.320
constitution that can still make a difference
to how

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.179
religion is viewed. I mean, for example--

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:10.879
I don't know, and you tell me, but--

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:15.319
I think if you're an American presidential
candidate and stood up and said, "I don't

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:18.599
believe in God,"

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:19.439
you'd probably find it--

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.600
I mean your campaign manager would say,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.079
"I don't think you should say that-- not--"

00:02:24.079 --> 00:02:27.620
Your strategist and your pollster would be leaping
out of their seats.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.690
So, you know,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.879
even if it's not-- you know,

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:36.549
sometimes religion can have an influence
outside of the formal nature

00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:37.560
of-- of

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.099
the debate

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:42.679
in terms of the constitution. But the classic
divide--

00:02:42.679 --> 00:02:47.899
the religious people saying, "We decide," and the secular
liberals saying, "Get out of the public realm,"

00:02:47.899 --> 00:02:51.560
I personally think you can move on from
that, really.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.349
And--

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:55.200
because what is clear that--

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.579
as Nick has just said--

00:02:57.579 --> 00:03:00.079
the democracy, pluralist democracy--

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.409
or, let me say this-- actually a pluralist
society,

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:08.139
because I think some of these things apply
even to non-democratic societies

00:03:08.139 --> 00:03:10.199
that are pluralist--

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:13.479
they require a common space

00:03:13.479 --> 00:03:16.450
where difference can be accommodated,

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.579
but where certain values or norms are shared. 

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:23.239
Right, so that people are respected

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:24.869
irrespective

00:03:24.869 --> 00:03:26.389
of their 

00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:27.780
religious commitment--

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.479
whatever their own religion says about certain
issues,

00:03:31.479 --> 00:03:35.199
nonetheless they accept, they come on a common
space at a certain point,

00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:42.199
and have to expect that public face in
the valleys and norms of-- within it.

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:47.399
So, I think most people understand that, even if they're
strongly religious, actually.

00:03:47.399 --> 00:03:49.169
Most people.

00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:52.109
Not everyone, but I think most do.

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:56.859
And also-- and I think this is where the
thing has moved on from

00:03:56.859 --> 00:03:58.019
Rawls's time--

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:00.319
although there are people who still

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:02.990
come up with some quite intemperate stuff on
this-- but

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.680
I think most people

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.259
believe also that religion can't be excluded

00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:08.330
from the public realm. 

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.890
In other words, it's one thing to say,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.819
"I, as a religious thinker--

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:13.049
I'm going to determine

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:16.349
the public realm." It's quite another thing to
say, "I simply want to be--

00:04:16.349 --> 00:04:18.430
my voice to be heard."

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.829
Now that, at its minimal, I think most people would

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:24.520
say nowadays, "Well, yeah, of course that's true."

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.629
And incidentally,

00:04:26.629 --> 00:04:28.129
the fact is-- which I would

00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:30.469
make for a practical point of view--

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:33.749
if you are of religious belief, 

00:04:33.749 --> 00:04:36.989
in a sense you can't

00:04:36.989 --> 00:04:41.430
exclude it; it defines you. So
there is no way that you could have a 

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.789
public debate in which your religious

00:04:43.789 --> 00:04:46.659
is not somehow

00:04:46.659 --> 00:04:48.069
relevant to you, and therefore relevant

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:50.650
to the debate that is occurring within

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.680
the public realm. So

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.830
my view is that

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.460
this debate is not

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.749
really about

00:05:02.749 --> 00:05:04.709
liberal versus religious,

00:05:04.709 --> 00:05:07.559
in the sense of

00:05:07.559 --> 00:05:11.710
the way that, say, O'Donovan uses it, whereas
the liberal is really a sort ofa

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.469
contractarian-- I think he said that, is that the right--

00:05:13.469 --> 00:05:15.520
the expression is that it's to do simply

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.270
with the contract--

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.580
whereas the religious people are the people
with the values.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.690
That is almost the suggestion I found 

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:25.999
in that writing.

00:05:25.999 --> 00:05:28.419
It seems to me that

00:05:28.419 --> 00:05:30.729
even if you are--

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:32.930
if you take

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.560
a non-religious view,

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.569
of

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:40.599
human rights,

00:05:40.599 --> 00:05:41.120
nonetheless--

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.110
as, indeed,

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.370
Rawls was pointing out--

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.299
behind any constitution of rights,

00:05:47.299 --> 00:05:49.690
there will lie

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.620
certain ideals, and those ideals are inspired by

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.439
certain

00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:54.220
values.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.819
So, it isn't really true to say that the

00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:58.409
person who's

00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:00.340
a secular liberal, 

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.270
and says, "Look, religion shouldn't come into
this debate," is really arguing from the point

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.689
of view of saying,

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:08.479
"I think this is just a personal contractual
issue;

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:11.500
it's got nothing to do with larger society."

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.730
In the same way,

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.789
as I say, that in my view most

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:17.839
people who are religious

00:06:17.839 --> 00:06:19.100
do in fact accept

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.650
that there is a common space and a common

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:23.930
constitution written or unwritten

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.619
to which they must conform.

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.999
In addition to that,

00:06:28.999 --> 00:06:31.590
I think that no one seriously

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:36.479
thinks nowadays that society doesn't require
certain values to underpin it.

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:39.270
Whether you're religious or you're secular.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.369
Most people talk about certain values

00:06:42.369 --> 00:06:44.330
that lie behind

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:48.669
whatever is the constitutional, the legal laws
to which people are expected to--

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:51.509
to conform.

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:53.849
And even the most non-religious

00:06:53.849 --> 00:06:57.759
politicians, certainly, in societies like ours--

00:06:57.759 --> 00:07:00.999
you know, I think

00:07:00.999 --> 00:07:04.659
they-- they're not merely motivated by certain values,
but they see them--

00:07:04.659 --> 00:07:07.879
see themselves as representing certain values.

00:07:07.879 --> 00:07:10.949
And

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:12.199
I think the people

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:14.539
are perfectly prepared,

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:14.960
mostly

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.789
to see

00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:19.530
those who are of religious belief

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.819
at least having the right to participate
in the debate

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:26.309
and the right to say-- because how can they
say otherwise--

00:07:26.309 --> 00:07:28.860
that their view of a particular debate

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:35.860
is motivated by their religious belief.

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.629
So I think it's really--

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:39.660
when we come to this

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.620
supposedly very strong divide

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:43.389
between

00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:46.509
the secular and the religious,

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:49.400
I think most people have resolved

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.069
that debate in their own mind. I'm not saying that
there aren't still people at the extremes on either side

00:07:54.069 --> 00:07:55.150
who will say

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.699
on the religious side, "I want to determine
the way that society works;	

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:01.280
I'm not prepared to accept,

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.639
my religion impels me

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:08.560
to act in a way inconsistent with the constitution"--
as Nick was explaining-- I'm sure there are people like that, 

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.900
there are people like that--

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.909
there are people on the other side

00:08:11.909 --> 00:08:14.099
as you saw with the

00:08:14.099 --> 00:08:17.729
statement from Jacoby, which is very much, you
know, just--

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:22.179
"Get out of there, you shouldn't be anywhere
near this debate"-- but I don't--

00:08:22.179 --> 00:08:23.800
I think most people

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:24.860
accept

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:29.280
that society's based on certain values, and
some of those values will derive from religion and

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:36.280
some of those values won't, but there's a place,
often, that is common and overlapping.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.000
I think the more

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.030
interesting questions now

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:42.210
are really

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:47.220
these, however:

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.390
in order to make

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.280
a society

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.440
work effectively,

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.650
what is it

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:56.570
that is necessary

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:57.590
for

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.940
religious and non-religious people to agree
upon

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.100
in order for that society to function well?

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.060
Now I agree with the two principles that

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:13.060
Nick has just outlined, and let me try and
put this in my own way

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.240
and develop it somewhat.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:18.339
I think,

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:20.410
first of all, that

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:23.950
people who are secular

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.340
need to understand also

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.340
that the religious dimension

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.310
can give a

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.100
quality

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.410
of a moral nature

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:38.130
to the debate within society

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.270
that can be

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:43.000
helpful and constructive and progressive.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.350
So, for example, I would say that of

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.190
the debates about

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:51.630
Western policy in relation, let's say, to
Africa and the third world debt--

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:55.540
the impact of those inspired by religion had

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.620
a significant impact

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:04.190
on the political decisions that were then
taken by leaders. I would say that the G8, which I

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.399
presided over in 2005, would never have
come to its conclusions

00:10:07.399 --> 00:10:09.850
without a very strong

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.140
religious movement

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.300
here, back in Europe, and

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.450
elsewhere in support of
that.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:23.630
And actually one of the big changes in the
whole issue to do with HIV/AIDS and how it

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.330
was

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:26.860
treated in Africa

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.240
was when

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.250
many of the Christian movements here

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.999
decided to get over

00:10:32.999 --> 00:10:34.820
various of the

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:39.110
sexual issues in relation to HIV/AIDS and actually
embrace the notion that it was an important

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.580
part of their

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:41.530
religious

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.410
duty in order to deal with this--

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.430
this issue, and

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.670
in fact,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:53.530
much of the health care that is delivered
in Africa today is delivered by religious organizations.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:55.590
So I think--

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:56.410
and I would--

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:57.570
it is a

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:02.180
very controversial idea to raise
this in--

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:07.140
if you take an issue like abortion, which is
very very controversial

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.900
in my society, and even more so in your society,

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:17.460
leave aside whether you agree or disagree
with the positions of religious people on it--

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:21.250
but I think that

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.370
it's not unhelpful

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:27.790
to actually have issues that are difficult
like that raise,

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:33.080
in a context that is spiritually or religiously

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:38.500
defined-- now that doesn't mean to say that
you end up with the same position of--

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.580
of, let's say, the Catholic church on that
issue or not.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.200
But I think

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.850
even secular people should recognize

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.460
that it's a debate that's still important to have,

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:53.470
and it's a debate that might not have taken
place in the same way unless

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.350
religious people had been prepared to put it on
the agenda.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.510
Now, likewise, I think that once

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.989
society comes to a view,

00:12:00.989 --> 00:12:03.170
that view has to be accepted--

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.410
by the religious people also--

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:07.239
but all I'm saying is there are--

00:12:07.239 --> 00:12:10.570
what I think the second people need to understand
is

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:12.480
that there can be

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.320
a way of raising questions-- that's why I do agree
with O'Donovan-- a way of raising

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.610
questions

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:24.750
and actually the establishment of an agenda
in which religious and spiritual thought

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:26.529
can play part, and play a positive part--

00:12:26.529 --> 00:12:30.480
because otherwise some issues would not
get raised at all. 

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.670
And--

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:33.500
you know, I think, for example,

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:35.520
of the work done by many

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.530
church organizations in relation

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:41.480
to the disabled. Now it's true there are many people
who are not religious at all who raise these

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.250
issues, but I would have to say,

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.020
certainly in my society, the most extraordinary

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:51.100
and selfless work is done by religious organizations
in this sphere. So.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.970
That's the first thing.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.369
The second thing, however--

00:12:55.369 --> 00:12:57.029
and I think the concomitant

00:12:57.029 --> 00:12:58.900
obligation

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:02.640
is the people who

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.510
are religious

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:06.720
also

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.990
need to understand

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:13.240
that those who do not

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:16.520
conform to a view derived from religious
belief

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.120
nonetheless have equal validity to state that
belief,

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:22.210
adhere to it, and advocate it.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:23.630
And

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:25.120
this is where,

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:31.110
if you like the concept of faith-friendly
democracy, or

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:32.600
democracy-friendly faith,

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:38.610
it's so important-- you need that balance
if you're going to achieve 

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:45.610
a proper settlement of the place of religion
within society.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.350
But I think this is not just about democracy

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:54.100
and that's-- I just want to make this amendment,
in a sense, to our--

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:57.530
our discussion.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:01.320
I think it is

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.690
possible

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:09.490
to have a non-democratic-- certainly in the sense of
non-democratic in a Western sense--

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:11.220
political system

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:14.330
that nonetheless is pluralist and nonetheless
has a--

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:15.800
the same

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.530
relationship that I'm describing

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:19.130
between religious faith

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:23.770
and politics.

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.670
What is essential

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.430
is the notion of pluralism.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:30.900
If you don't have the exact--

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.630
the question of pluralism,

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:34.460
the issue of pluralism there,

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.550
that concept rooted in the society--

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:42.150
then I think it's very difficult.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:45.180
And so my view is that

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.290
this very--

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:51.020
what I would call quite extreme, almost a slightly
old-fashioned sense

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.949
of a divide between

00:14:52.949 --> 00:14:54.420
those people who say,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.670
"My religion entitles me to enforce my
view against you,"

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.890
and those people who say, "Actually, you

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:03.170
take your religious views out of here; we don't
want anything to do with that,

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:06.220
and it's not a legitimate basis upon which you
can put your case,"--

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:08.280
I think that is largely--

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:13.140
people-- as I say, I'm not saying that it doesn't
exist, but I don't think that's what most people

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.210
find difficult today.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.290
Most people can accept

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:21.470
that religious people have a right to
express their view, most religious people 

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:24.680
can accept that they don't have the 
right to enforce it.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:30.770
I think the question is, though, what are the conditions

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:31.589
that--

00:15:31.589 --> 00:15:33.090
that give rise

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.430
to

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:34.939
that

00:15:34.939 --> 00:15:36.770
faith-friendly

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:39.650
society and a society-friendly faith.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:43.440
And those-- in my view-- are,

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.180
as I say, that the secular needs to understand
the religious connections

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.110
enrich

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:50.670
the social and moral dialogue of a nation,

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.440
and the religious need to understand that

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.340
those who aren't religious have equal validity
in their views.

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:59.699
The question, to my mind, then becomes the practical
question

00:15:59.699 --> 00:16:04.230
of how you best create such societies.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:05.770
And here's

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:06.980
where I think

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:10.950
the political and the social and the faith
conditions

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:12.570
for such societies are

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.959
an interesting subject of analysis and

00:16:15.959 --> 00:16:17.100
discussion, because--

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.139
and it may be it's just because of

00:16:19.139 --> 00:16:21.280
my background-- I kind of

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:24.129
think it's interesting to analyze the question
and

00:16:24.129 --> 00:16:26.229
even more interesting to

00:16:26.229 --> 00:16:29.030
find an answer to the question--

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:30.300
and so,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.850
what is it

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:35.140
that-- that gives rise to this?

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.910
Because I can describe and just have what are
the attitudes, but how do you encourage

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:40.160
those attitudes?

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:46.550
How do you abet those attitudes?

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:49.780
And here's where I think that the--

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.280
the policy,

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:54.990
in a sense, that flows from this,

00:16:54.990 --> 00:17:00.020
is as follows: I think,

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:02.580
to begin with,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.880
there does need to be

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:07.180
some clear 

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:07.549
constitutional

00:17:07.549 --> 00:17:12.370
or legal framework within which this is set,

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:14.530
and the full principles that Nick outlined,

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:18.660
they're behind the US Constitution.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.120
Those

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:24.560
undoubtedly helped create

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:29.060
the sense in the US today that there
is a legitimate and an illegitimate way of having

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:30.630
the debate between

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:34.130
religion and politics.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:36.510
So I think that is important.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.180
I think secondly,

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:43.020
none of this

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.480
would work without

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:48.800
action to support it. I think this-- what we're
talking about is radical implications, for

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.470
example, for our education system,

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:54.090
and I think in particular the way that religion is taught

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:56.400
within our education system

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:59.760
within civic society.

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.370
Within international engagement-- for example,

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:05.750
the alliance of civilizations concept put
forward by Turkey 

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:08.580
and Spain--

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:13.000
in some process of development now-- is
actually important

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.720
in this regard.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:17.990
And so if we want to create that society

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.200
in which there is the balance

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.670
of the proper space for religion,

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:25.200
to make its view clear,

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:26.980
but also the--

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.179
the broader polity that must encompass more
than the religious--

00:18:30.179 --> 00:18:33.120
I think it requires

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.020
that constitutional framework,

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:36.620
it requires policy

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:39.400
on behalf of government that supports

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.480
such attitudes,

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:45.220
and the third thing that it needs, and this is--

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.760
which touches directly on our

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.790
faith and globalization course--

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:57.810
is it does require a certain attitude of mind.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.710
And this is where I find it--

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:03.070
it's quite interesting just listening to

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:05.679
Nick's analysis of the

00:19:05.679 --> 00:19:06.900
way that

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:09.020
the intellectual debate has

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:11.850
gone.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.670
In the end, what this requires is

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:19.910
what I would call an open rather than a closed 
attitude

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:25.870
to both the process of globalization in the society
in which we live,

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:28.400
and how you inculcate that attitude

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:29.970
has also got to be part

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:30.660
of the

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:37.660
the discourse in and around this subject.

00:19:37.789 --> 00:19:41.570
Because, you see, ultimately,

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:44.840
in order for this balance to work,

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:48.790
in order for us to be able to say, as Nick has
really

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:52.060
outlined for you, that

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.490
you should not exclude

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:58.100
the religious, the religious has a place and
a voice, but nonetheless that is within certain

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:00.280
rules and with certain

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:01.030
qualifications

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:04.820
to how that voice should be spoken--

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.009
in order to do that

00:20:06.009 --> 00:20:09.510
and in order to have a society work like that,

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.520
there needs to be within the people in that
society

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:15.480
a certain openness to the other.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.640
So that, for example,

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:20.300
someone who is religious

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:21.500
is not merely

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:25.860
not exclusivist toward with those of other religions,

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.760
but is actually prepared to acknowledge that
the secular can sometimes have

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:34.160
something to say to the religious world.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.400
But it also requires-- and actually
this is--

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:40.590
has often, I think,

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:43.159
far more resisted-- that those that are secular

00:20:43.159 --> 00:20:47.040
should also realize that those who are religiously
inspired

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:48.280
have something

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:54.000
to offer and to add to

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.170
the forum within which political debate

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.059
is conducted.

00:20:58.059 --> 00:21:00.010
So, to summarize,

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:02.350
my view is this:

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:03.840
that

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:07.390
this is--

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:10.520
I think there is a developing consensus

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:11.960
that you do require

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.850
faith-friendly

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:15.690
democracy, democracy-friendly faith, or

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:17.270
faith-friendly society,

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:19.210
society-friendly faith--

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.470
the question is how you

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.559
achieve such a

00:21:23.559 --> 00:21:25.230
thing, and in order to achieve it,

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:27.860
you need a combination of

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:30.240
the right constitutional framework

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:32.050
the right policy

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:32.850
and the right attitude.

